      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Certificate No: R49/2006-DE1-14.01\n- Scheme: B (OIML-CS certificates of conformity are scheme B... actually R49/2006 predates OIML-CS, but per the schema we should give scheme A|B. The certificate format here is the pre-OIML-CS format. Hmm. The instruction says \"scheme (A|B)\". Typically OIML-CS certificates issued under scheme B are conformity certificates. This is an older-style certificate. Actually, historically these \"OIML Certificates of Conformity\" were just \"OIML certificates\" — the scheme A/B distinction came with OIML CS (2017?). I'll set scheme to \"B\" since it's a Certificate of Conformity issued by an issuing authority... Actually, for these older certificates, they're typically considered scheme B-like (certificate of conformity). Hmm, but R 49-1/-2/-3 (2006) edition — the certificate includes test reports, issuing authority, CIML member sign-off — that's the classic OIML Certificate of Conformity system which is now Scheme B. I'll use \"B\".\n- project_number: not present\n- page_total: 11\n- member_state: Germany\n- date_issued: 08.10.2014 → \"2014-10-08\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present (there's \"R3-0033\" but that's unclear — probably a PTB internal number; skip)\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\n- address_lines: [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"]\n- person_responsible: Dr. G. Wendt\n- person_title: Head of Department\n- CIML member: Dr. R. Schwartz, Vice-President — not part of issuing authority per schema; could be omitted or... The schema doesn't have a field for CIML member. Omit.\n- phone/email/website: not present\n\n**Applicants:**\n- name: Diehl Metering GmbH\n- address_lines: [\"Industriestraße 13\", \"91522 Ansbach\", \"GERMANY\"]\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- \"Manufacturer of the certified type is the applicant.\" → same as applicant: Diehl Metering GmbH.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\" (the example list has \"Gas meter\"; water meter fits)\n- type_designations: [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"]\n- module_designation: not applicable / not mentioned. It's a water meter with mechanical indicating device — complete device, not a module. I'd say null or \"Not applicable\". Since not mentioned, omit or null. I'll put null... Actually the schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Since it's a complete water meter, \"Not applicable\" makes sense. But rule 8 says don't fabricate. I'll omit it (or null). Let me include \"Not applicable\"? Hmm — the certificate doesn't discuss modules. I'll omit it to be safe. Actually, giving null is fine per schema. I'll just omit.\n- description: \"Water meter with mechanical indicating device\"\n\n**Model family:** The cert covers 4 types (ET1–ET4) × DN15/DN20 × Q3 values (1.6, 2.5, 4.0 m³/h) × Q3/Q1 ratios × temperature classes (cold 0.1–30 °C, hot 30–90 °C) × orientation (H vs rising/falling + V90°/V45°). This is a rich family. The models are best identified by combination: e.g., \"ET1-ET3-ET4 DN15 Q3=2.5m³/h (T30, H)\".\n\nThis is a lot of tables. Let me enumerate the flow-range tables:\n\nPage 3:\n1. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 8 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125; Q1 = 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025, 0.020; Q2 = 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.032; Q3 = 2.5; Q4 = 3.13; Q2/Q1 = 1.6. Accuracy ±2%/±5%, temp 0.1–30°C, Orientation: H.\n2. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** (again, same header) — 4 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50; Q1 = 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050; etc. Accuracy ±2%/±5%, temp 0.1–30°C, Orientation: rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°.\n\nWait, the header is identical for the second one but orientation differs. Interesting — the second table with fewer columns is for vertical orientation. So the Q3/Q1 ratio range differs by orientation: horizontal gets full range (25–125), vertical gets only 25–50.\n\nPage 4:\n3. **ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 7 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100; Q1 = 0.100...0.025; Q2 = 0.160...0.040. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, H.\n4. **ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 2 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5; ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n5. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 7 columns: Q3/Q1 = 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80; Q1 = 0.080...0.020; Q2 = 0.128...0.032; Q4 = 2.00. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, H.\n\nPage 5:\n6. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 20, 25, 31.5; Q1 = 0.080, 0.064, 0.051; Q2 = 0.128, 0.102, 0.081. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n7. **ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 7 columns (last empty): Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63; Q1 = 0.100...0.025; Q2 = 0.160...0.041; Q4 = 2.00. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, H.\n8. **ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 2 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20; Q1 = 0.100, 0.080; Q2 = 0.160, 0.128. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n\nPage 6:\n9. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 6 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50; Q1 = 0.16...0.050; Q2 = 0.25...0.080; Q4 = 3.13. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, H.\n10. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25; Q1 = 0.16, 0.13, 0.10; Q2 = 0.25, 0.20, 0.16. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n11. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h** — 6 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80; Q1 = 0.16...0.05; Q2 = 0.26...0.08; Q4 = 5.00. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, H.\n\nPage 7:\n12. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40; Q1 = 0.16, 0.13, 0.10; Q2 = 0.26, 0.20, 0.16. ±2%/±5%, 0.1–30°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n13. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 7 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100; Q1 = 0.100...0.025; Q2 = 0.160...0.040; Q4 = 3.13. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot water)\n14. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 4 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n\nPage 8:\n15. **ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 6 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot)\n16. **ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 2 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n17. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 7 columns: Q3/Q1 = 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot)\n18. (page 9 first) **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 20, 25, 31.5; Q1 = 0.08, 0.06, 0.05; Q2 = 0.13, 0.10, 0.08. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n\nPage 9:\n19. **ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 7 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot)\n20. **ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h** — 2 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n\nPage 10:\n21. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 6 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot)\n22. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 16, 20, 25. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n23. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h** — 6 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, H. (hot)\n\nPage 11:\n24. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h** — 3 columns: Q3/Q1 = 25, 31.5, 40. ±3%/±5%, 30–90°C, rising/falling V90/V45.\n\nThen pressure range table:\n- Pmin: 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa), Pmax: 10 bar (1.0 MPa)\n- ΔP per type: DN15 Q3 1.6 → ΔP 25; DN15 Q3 2.5 → ΔP 63; DN20 Q3 2.5 → ΔP 40; DN20 Q3 4.0 → ΔP 63.\n- Minimum straight length inlet/outlet: 0 mm / 0 mm.\n\nOK. So this certificate covers a large model family. How should I structure this?\n\nThe natural model identity: combination of (type designation(s), DN, Q3, temperature class/accuracy class, orientation). Each table row-column combination corresponds to a specific Q3/Q1 ratio variant, which is actually a distinct metrological configuration: for each Q3/Q1 ratio, Q1 and Q2 are determined.\n\nThe cleanest approach given the schema:\n\n- model_family.models[]: each model = a specific variant. I could define model_id as e.g. \"ET1/ET3/ET4-DN15-Q3=2.5m³/h-R25\" (ratio 25). That gives ~100+ models. That's a lot but systematic.\n\nAlternatively, models at the level of tables: model_id like \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_H\" and treat Q3/Q1 ratio as a config axis. That's better: 24 model entries (or fewer, since some tables repeat headers).\n\nActually let me think about how to present this in a way that is faithful and useful:\n\nOption A: model per table (24 models), with matrix_tables capturing the flow tables, and model_level characteristics capturing q3, dn, accuracy classes, temperature range, orientation.\n\nOption B: model per ratio variant. Too granular.\n\nI'll go with Option A but merge duplicates? The headers repeat: e.g., \"ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5\" appears on page 3 (H, 8 cols; V, 4 cols), page 7 (H, 7 cols; V, 4 cols). The H tables on page 3 and page 7 differ: page 3 has 8 ratio columns (25–125), page 7 has 7 ratio columns (25–100). Hmm, that's odd — same nominal model (ET1,ET3,ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5, H, hot?) — wait no: page 3 is cold (±2%, 0.1–30°C), page 7 is hot (±3%, 30–90°C). So the cold horizontal version covers ratios 25–125, while the hot horizontal version covers 25–100. Similarly page 5 table 6 (cold V) has ratios 20,25,31.5 while page 9 table 18 (hot V) has 20,25,31.5 — same values, but different temp class.\n\nSo each of the 24 tables is a distinct model/configuration variant. The temperature class (cold: 0.1–30°C, class 2; hot: 30–90°C, class 3) is an attribute per model. Orientation (H vs rising/falling V90/V45) is per model too.\n\nSo model_id could be like: \"ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3=2.5m³/h cold H\". Hmm, but temperature ranges aren't officially \"cold/hot\" — I can label them.\n\nLet me define model_id scheme: `{types}_DN{dn}_Q3={q3}m³/h_T{T/30}__{orientation}` — getting complicated. Simpler: use a descriptive label.\n\nActually, maybe a cleaner approach: since tables are per (type-group, DN, Q3, temp-class, orientation), define models:\n\n1. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80,100,125\n2. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 25,31.5,40,50\n3. ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80,100\n4. ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 25,31.5\n5. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 20,25,31.5,40,50,63,80\n6. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 20,25,31.5\n7. ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 16,20,25,31.5,40,50,63\n8. ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 16,20\n9. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 16,20,25,31.5,40,50\n10. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 16,20,25\n11. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0m³/h_0.1-30°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80\n12. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0m³/h_0.1-30°C_V — ratios 25,31.5,40\n13. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80,100\n14. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 25,31.5,40,50\n15. ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80\n16. ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 25,31.5\n17. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 20,25,31.5,40,50,63,80\n18. ET1/ET3/ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 20,25,31.5\n19. ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 16,20,25,31.5,40,50,63\n20. ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 16,20\n21. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 16,20,25,31.5,40,50\n22. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 16,20,25\n23. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0m³/h_30-90°C_H — ratios 25,31.5,40,50,63,80\n24. ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0m³/h_30-90°C_V — ratios 25,31.5,40\n\nThat's 24 models. \n\nFor model_level characteristics, per model:\n- nominal_diameter (DN): 15 or 20, unit \"mm\"\n- q3 (permanent flowrate): 1.6/2.5/4.0, m³/h\n- q4 (overload flowrate): 3.13/2.00/3.13/5.00, m³/h\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 (all)\n- accuracy class: 2 (cold) or 3 (hot) — \"accuracy_class\" attribute\n- water_temperature_range: {min, max} °C\n- orientation: \"H\" or \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- types applicable: which of ET1–ET4\n\nBut per ratio variant, q1 and q2 vary. So q1/q2 depend on both model AND ratio (a config axis \"q3_q1_ratio\"). That's config_level!\n\ncharacteristics.config_level[]:\n- attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"q3_q1_ratio\", values: [{condition: \"ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3=2.5m³/h 0.1-30°C H, ratio 25\", value: 0.100}, ...] — that's very verbose, 100+ entries.\n\nAlternatively, put q1, q2, q3/q1 ratio combos in matrix_tables (preserve the raw tables) and only put per-model attrs in model_level. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The flow range tables are exactly that: rows Q1..Q3/Q1 × columns per ratio.\n\nI think the best structure:\n- matrix_tables: 24 flow-range tables (or maybe I can consolidate?). Each table: name, columns [\"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\"], rows: one per ratio column with q1, q2, q3, q4 values. That transforms the transposed layout into row-per-variant. And link each table to its model via the name.\n\nHmm, but the schema for matrix_tables has columns, rows, description, footnotes. I can put the model context in the name/description.\n\nAlso the pressure table → matrix_tables entry with rows per (DN, Q3): ΔP value.\n\nLet me now also decide model_level entries. With 24 models, each attribute gets 24 values. Attributes:\n- dn (nominal diameter, mm): values per model\n- q3: m³/h per model\n- q4: m³/h per model\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 for all\n- max_admissible_error_upper_zone / accuracy class: Actually \"Accuracy class: ±2% (Q2≤Q≤Q4); ±5% (Q1≤Q<Q2)\". This is R49 class 2 or 3. For model_level: attribute \"accuracy_class\" values: 2 or 3 (the ±% in upper zone). I might do two attributes: \"maximum_admissible_error_upper_zone\" (%) and \"maximum_admissible_error_lower_zone\" (%). Values 2/5 for cold, 3/5 for hot.\n- water_temperature_range: {min, max} °C per model\n- orientation: string per model\n- applicable types: list of types — could be an attribute with string value \"ET1, ET3, ET4\".\n\nAdditionally config_level for q1/q2 per ratio? Let me consider: config_level with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\" — but condition needs to identify the model too. The schema's config_level values: {condition, value}. I could make condition like \"model=<id>; ratio=25\". This becomes unwieldy. I think matrix_tables is the right place, and I'll keep config_level out, OR include one config_level entry per... hmm.\n\nActually, maybe a cleaner way: instead of 24 models, treat the *ratio variant* as the model! Each column in each table = a model variant with specific Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4. Then model_level characteristics q1, q2, q3, q4, dn, temp, orientation, ratio all per model. Number of models: count columns:\n\nTable 1: 8, T2: 4, T3: 7, T4: 2, T5: 7, T6: 3, T7: 7, T8: 2, T9: 6, T10: 3, T11: 6, T12: 3, T13: 7, T14: 4, T15: 6, T16: 2, T17: 7, T18: 3, T19: 7, T20: 2, T21: 6, T22: 3, T23: 6, T24: 3.\n\nSum: 8+4=12; +7=19; +2=21; +7=28; +3=31; +7=38; +2=40; +6=46; +3=49; +6=55; +3=58; +7=65; +4=69; +6=75; +2=77; +7=84; +3=87; +7=94; +2=96; +6=102; +3=105; +6=111; +3=114.\n\n114 model variants. That's a lot but it's systematic. model_id like \"ET1/ET3/ET4-DN15-Q3=2.5-T30-H-R25\". Hmm, this is heavy but arguably most faithful: each ratio is a distinct metrological variant (different Q1/Q2).\n\nWhich approach is better for the downstream consumer? The schema emphasizes model families where attributes vary per model. Given the tables, I think a hybrid:\n\n- models[] at the level of (type group, DN, Q3, temp, orientation) = 24 models.\n- matrix_tables[] preserving each flow table (24 entries), each with description naming the model.\n- model_level characteristics: dn, q3, q4, q2_over_q1, temperature range, accuracy class(es), orientation.\n\nAlternatively merge models across orientation (orientation as config axis) and across temp class? The temp class is a first-class attribute of a water meter (T30 vs T90). Orientation is an installation config. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with 24 models where orientation is part of model identity — no wait, orientation could be a config axis: same meter, installed horizontally OR vertically with limited range. When installed vertically, the approved ratio range is restricted. I think orientation as part of the model variant is fine since the approved flow ranges differ.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe simpler and cleaner — models at (type group, DN, Q3, temp class) level = 12 models (6 cold... let me count: cold: DN15 2.5 (ET1/3/4), DN15 2.5 (ET2), DN15 1.6 (ET1/3/4), DN15 1.6 (ET2), DN20 2.5, DN20 4.0 → 6; hot: same 6 → 12 models). Then orientation is a config axis: H (full ratio set) vs V (restricted ratio set). And the ratio itself is another config dimension with q1/q2 per ratio.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"orientation_and_q3_q1_ratio\"... this gets messy.\n\nHonestly, the matrix_tables approach preserves everything, and model_level gives per-variant attributes. For q1/q2 dependence on ratio, I could use config_level with axis \"flow_range_ratio (Q3/Q1)\" and conditions like \"model X, ratio 25\". Hmm.\n\nLet me just be pragmatic: The instructions say matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The flow tables fit matrix_tables. model_level for attributes per model. I'll define 24 models (including orientation) so q1/q2/ratio data lives in matrix_tables keyed by model via description/name.\n\nHmm wait — but then there are no config_level entries. That's acceptable.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. Maybe define models at the ratio-variant level but with compact model_ids, and put q1, q2 as model_level attributes. 114 models × several attributes — the JSON will be large but complete and machine-usable. E.g.:\n\nmodel_id: \"ET1.ET3.ET4-DN15-Q3_2.5-T90-H-R25\"... \n\nHmm. I think 114 models is excessive and error-prone; 24 models with matrix tables is cleaner and still complete. The matrix_tables rows carry q1/q2/q3/q4 per ratio.\n\nLet me structure each flow matrix_table:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Flow range — ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3=2.5 m³/h, 0.1–30 °C, horizontal\",\n  \"columns\": [\"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_q1_ratio\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 25, \"q1\": 0.100, \"q2\": 0.160, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_q1_ratio\": 1.6},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 31.5, \"q1\": 0.079, \"q2\": 0.127, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_q1_ratio\": 1.6},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 40, \"q1\": 0.063, \"q2\": 0.100, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_q1_ratio\": 1.6},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 50, \"q1\": 0.050, \"q2\": 0.080, ...},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 63, \"q1\": 0.040, \"q2\": 0.063, ...},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 80, \"q1\": 0.031, \"q2\": 0.050, ...},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 100, \"q1\": 0.025, \"q2\": 0.040, ...},\n    {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 125, \"q1\": 0.020, \"q2\": 0.032, ...}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Model: ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3=2.5m³/h, 0.1–30°C, H\"\n}\n\nAlso description can mention accuracy classes.\n\nLet me now carefully transcribe each table.\n\n**Table 1 (p3): ET1,ET3,ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5, cold, H**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025, 0.020\nQ2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.032\nQ3: 2.5 (all)\nQ4: 3.13 (all)\nQ2/Q1: 1.6 (all)\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125\n\n**Table 2 (p3): ET1,ET3,ET4, DN15, Q3 2.5, cold, V(rising/falling V90/V45)**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050\nQ2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40, 50\n\n**Table 3 (p4): ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5, cold, H**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025\nQ2: 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100\n\n**Table 4 (p4): ET2, DN15, Q3 2.5, cold, V**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.079\nQ2: 0.160, 0.127\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5\n\n**Table 5 (p4): ET1,ET3,ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6, cold, H**\nQ1: 0.080, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040, 0.032, 0.025, 0.020\nQ2: 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.05