15, Q3 1.6, hot, V**\nQ1: 0.08, 0.06, 0.05\nQ2: 0.13, 0.10, 0.08\nQ3/Q1: 20, 25, 31.5\n\n**Table 19 (p9): ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6, hot, H**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.080, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040, 0.032, 0.025\nQ2: 0.160, 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.051, 0.041\nQ4: 2.00\nQ3/Q1: 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63\n\n**Table 20 (p9): ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6, hot, V**\nQ1: 0.100, 0.080\nQ2: 0.160, 0.128\nQ3/Q1: 16, 20\n\n**Table 21 (p10): ET1-4, DN20, Q3 2.5, hot, H**\nQ1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.050\nQ2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.080\nQ4: 3.13\nQ3/Q1: 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50\n\n**Table 22 (p10): ET1-4, DN20, Q3 2.5, hot, V**\nQ1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10\nQ2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16\nQ3/Q1: 16, 20, 25\n\n**Table 23 (p10): ET1-4, DN20, Q3 4.0, hot, H**\nQ1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05\nQ2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08\nQ4: 5.00\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80\n\n**Table 24 (p11): ET1-4, DN20, Q3 4.0, hot, V**\nQ1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10\nQ2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16\nQ3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40\n\nNote table 7 has a trailing empty cell (8th col empty) — ignore.\n\nNow the pressure table:\nPmin 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa), Pmax 10 bar (1.0 MPa); ΔP per DN/Q3: DN15 Q3 1.6 → ΔP 25; DN15 Q3 2.5 → ΔP 63; DN20 Q3 2.5 → ΔP 40; DN20 Q3 4.0 → ΔP 63.\n\nΔP values: these are pressure-loss classes? In R49, Δp max... Actually \"ΔP 25\" likely means maximum pressure loss at Q3... Hmm, in water meter standards (ISO 4066/R49), pressure loss classes are Δp 25, 40, 63 (in bar ×10^-1? Actually pressure-loss classes: Δp ≤ 0.25 bar at Q3 = class 25; 0.40 bar = class 40; 0.63 bar = class 63). Yes! Pressure loss classes per ISO: ΔP class indicates max pressure loss in bar×... class 63 → 0.63 bar. So \"ΔP 25\" = pressure loss class 25 (max 0.25 bar at Q3). I'll record as string values \"ΔP 25\"? Or value 25 with unit null and attribute \"pressure_loss_class\". I'll record as matrix table + model_level attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" with value 25/40/63, unit null, per DN/Q3 combo. Since DN15 models come in both 1.6 and 2.5 with different classes, and DN20 too, this attribute maps to (dn, q3) which is captured in the matrix table.\n\nNote: ΔP applies to DN15 & DN20 regardless of temp class presumably — the table lists only DN/Q3, so it applies across all models with those DN/Q3. I'll note that in description.\n\nMinimum straight length inlet/outlet: 0 mm / 0 mm → type_level characteristic: minimum_straight_length_inlet: 0 mm; minimum_straight_length_outlet: 0 mm.\n\nType-level characteristics (whole certificate):\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- minimum_admissible_pressure: {value: 0.3, unit_symbol: \"bar\"} — also 0.03 MPa; record as 0.3 bar. Could add note.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 (all models) — could be type_level since constant! Yes: \"q2_over_q1: 1.6\" applies everywhere. I'll put it type_level.\n- q3_over_q1: varies per model/ratio — that's in matrix tables.\n- accuracy class varies (2 cold, 3 hot) → model_level.\n\nModel level attributes per the 24 models:\n- type_designations applicable: e.g. [\"ET1\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"] — as attribute \"applicable_types\" with string value \"ET1, ET3, ET4\"? Hmm, values must be structured objects with value/unit_symbol. A list isn't allowed per strict rules... value can be a string. I'll use string \"ET1, ET3, ET4\". Or omit since model_id encodes it. I'll include \"applicable_types\" as string to be explicit. Actually the model_id will encode types; including also as attribute is redundant but harmless. I'll include it — helps consumers. Hmm, but it inflates 24 entries × ... it's fine, one attribute with 24 values.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the model_id naming to be cleaner:\nUse e.g. \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_T30_H\" where T30 means 0.1–30°C (cold, class 2) and T90 for 30–90°C (hot, class 3). Hmm, T30/T90 is actually the official R49 temperature designations? In R49 water meters: T30 (0.1–30°C), T90 (30–90°C)? Actually temperature class T30: 0.1 °C to 30 °C; T90: 30 °C to 90 °C. Yes! R49-1 defines temperature classes T30, T90 etc. So the cold tables are T30 and hot tables are T90. I can use \"T30\"/\"T90\" in model ids and as attribute \"temperature_class\" with values \"T30\"/\"T90\", plus water_temperature_range.\n\nmodel_id: \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H\". Good.\n\nAnd V orientation: \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_V\". Orientation attribute value: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\". The original says \"Orientation: rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° und V 45°\" (\"und\" is German for \"and\"). I'll normalize \"und\" → \"and\".\n\nModel labels: human-friendly, e.g. \"ET1, ET3, ET4 — DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h, T30, horizontal\".\n\nNow model_level attribute list (each with 24 values):\n1. \"applicable_types\" — string\n2. \"nominal_diameter_dn\" — 15/20, unit \"mm\"\n3. \"q3\" — 1.6/2.5/4.0, unit \"m³/h\"\n4. \"q4\" — 2.0/3.13/5.0, unit \"m³/h\"\n5. \"temperature_class\" — \"T30\"/\"T90\"\n6. \"water_temperature_range\" — {min:0.1,max:30} or {min:30,max:90}, unit \"°C\"\n7. \"maximum_admissible_error_upper_zone\" — 2 or 3, unit \"%\" (for Q2≤Q≤Q4)\n8. \"maximum_admissible_error_lower_zone\" — 5, unit \"%\" (Q1≤Q<Q2)\n9. \"orientation\" — \"H\" or \"rising and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n10. \"pressure_loss_class\"? This varies by (dn,q3): DN15/1.6→25, DN15/2.5→63, DN20/2.5→40, DN20/4.0→63. Could include as model_level with 24 values. Since the pressure table lists per DN/Q3 without temp/orientation distinctions, it applies to all. Include as model_level \"pressure_loss_class\" — good.\n\nType level: q2_over_q1 = 1.6 (dimensionless, applies to all). Also min/max pressure, straight lengths.\n\nAlso, the flow tables themselves → matrix_tables with per-model descriptions. And the pressure table → matrix_tables.\n\nAlso \"model_family\": family_name — no explicit family name in cert. The type designations ET1..ET4; family could be named null. family_name: null.\n\nmodels[]: 24 entries with model_id and label.\n\nNow, is 24 matrix tables + models too much? It's thorough. Let me consider merging the H/V orientation tables into one table per (types, DN, Q3, temp) with an orientation column? The ratio sets differ (H: up to 125; V: up to 50). A merged table with an \"orientation\" column and one row per (ratio, orientation):\n\nrows: [{\"q3_q1_ratio\": 25, \"q1\": 0.100, \"q2\": 0.160, \"orientation\": \"H\"}, {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 25, ..., \"orientation\": \"V\"}, ...]. That halves the tables to 12. But deviates more from source layout. I'll keep 24 tables matching source pages, each clearly labeled. Actually, 24 tables is a lot of JSON but fine.\n\nHmm, alternatively, I could reduce: since V tables are subsets of H tables (V ratio set ⊂ H ratio set, with same q1/q2), e.g., table 2 (V: ratios 25,31.5,40,50) is subset of table 1 (H: 25–125) — check q1 values: same (0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050). Table 4 (V: 25, 31.5) ⊂ table 3 (H). Table 18 (V hot: ratios 20,25,31.5; q1 0.08,0.06,0.05) vs table 17 (H hot: 20,25,31.5,40,...; q1 0.080, 0.064, 0.051) — values slightly differ in precision (0.06 vs 0.064? table 18 Q1: 0.08, 0.06, 0.05; table 17: 0.080, 0.064, 0.051). Interesting, rounding differences. And table 12 vs 11: table 12 Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10 = table 11's first three. OK.\n\nI'll preserve all 24 as separate tables — faithful.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe combine per (types,DN,Q3,temp) into a single table with orientation column — 12 tables. This reduces redundancy. But then model_level orientation attribute is per orientation... models are still 24. Eh. I'll do 24 matrix tables, one per model, keeping rows per ratio. Actually wait — with 24 models and 24 tables, each table maps 1:1 to a model. Alternatively, I can put the flow ranges directly... there's no \"model_characteristics\" free-form field other than model_level attributes, which require the {value, unit_symbol} shape per model — a list of q1 values per model isn't allowed. So matrix_tables it is.\n\nLet me now also handle \"accuracy class\" more carefully. R49-1 (2006) accuracy classes are 1 and 2, where class 2 = ±2%/±5%? Hmm wait: In R49 (2006), maximum admissible errors: ±5% in lower zone (Q1≤Q<Q2), ±2% in upper zone (Q2≤Q≤Q4) for class 2; ±3% upper for class... Actually R49-1:2006 defines accuracy class 1 and 2: class 1: ±3%/±1%? Hmm, no wait: class 1: lower ±3% upper ±1%; class 2: lower ±5% upper ±2%. But this cert shows ±3% upper for hot water meters. For hot water (T90), MPE upper zone ±3%? The cert explicitly states ±3% (Q2≤Q≤Q4) and ±5% (Q1≤Q<Q2). That corresponds to accuracy class 3 in the table? Actually R49-1:2006 Table: Accuracy class 1: ±5%/±3%?? Let me not overthink — the certificate text explicitly gives percentages. I'll record exactly:\n\n- \"maximum_admissible_error_upper_zone\": value 2 or 3, unit \"%\"\n- \"maximum_admissible_error_lower_zone\": value 5, unit \"%\"\n\nAnd avoid claiming class numbers (the cert doesn't state \"class 2\"/\"class 3\" explicitly). Good — don't fabricate.\n\nHmm, wait — should accuracy class be in recommendation.accuracy_classes? The schema: recommendation: {id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]}. The recommendations here are R 49-1, R 49-2, R 49-3, each edition 2006. The canonical id: \"R49\". There are three parts. I could list one recommendation entry with id \"R49\"? Or three entries? The schema shows a single recommendation object, not an array. Hmm. \"recommendation: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]\".\n\nCertificate references R 49-1, R 49-2, R 49-3 all edition 2006. I'll emit one recommendation object: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"scheme\": \"B\"} and maybe note parts in... no field for parts. Hmm, could I add \"parts\"? Rule 8 says don't fabricate, but adding non-schema fields? The schema is layered; I'll stick to schema. Actually, maybe I can use id \"R49\" and mention parts nowhere. Alternatively \"id\": \"R49-1/-2/-3\"? The instruction says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". So \"R49\". Edition: 2006.\n\naccuracy_classes: the MPE-based classes... The cert doesn't label \"accuracy class\" numerically beyond percentages. Hmm, \"Accuracy class: ±2 %...\" — the cert literally labels the percentages as \"Accuracy class\". For water meters, classes would be 2 (cold) and 3 (hot)? Given R49-1:2006 accuracy classes are 1 and 2 only, and ±3% upper isn't a standard class... Actually for combined meters? Hmm. Actually R49-1:2006 does define temperature classes T30...T180 and accuracy classes 1, 2. MPE for class 2: higher zone ±2%, lower ±5%. For hot water meters T90 at class 2? But cert says ±3% for hot. Hmm — R49-1:2006 Table 1: MPE high zone: class 1: ±3%? Let me recall... OIML R49-1:2006: \"Accuracy class 1: MPE: high zones ±1%, low zones ±3%; Accuracy class 2: high ±2%, low ±5%.\" And for hot water... Hmm, but there's also \"temperature class\" effect? I don't recall ±3% high zone for class 2 hot. Actually, I think R49-1 has Table 2 with classes 1 and 2 for cold, and for hot water: class 1: ±2%? Not sure. The certificate says ±3% for the hot ones. I won't guess class numbers. In recommendation.accuracy_classes, I could omit or leave empty. Since not explicitly stated numerically, omit accuracy_classes. Hmm, but schema lists it... omitting is allowed (rule 8).\n\nWait, actually — maybe I should include accuracy_classes: [2, 3]? The cert's \"Accuracy class: ±2%\" strongly implies water meter accuracy class 2 (cold) and class 3 (hot)... but R49-1:2006 doesn't have class 3. Hmm, but for hot water meters R49-1:2006 4.2.4: \"Hot water meters: accuracy class... MPE ±3%\"? Hmm, I genuinely am unsure. Safest: don't emit accuracy_classes since it's ambiguous. Actually, let me include accuracy_classes as [\"2\", \"3\"]? No — don't fabricate. Omit.\n\nEdition: 2006 (int).\n\n**Scheme**: These pre-2017 certificates aren't formally A or B, but structure-wise this is the classic OIML Certificate of Conformity = the ancestor of Scheme B. I'll use \"B\".\n\n**project_number**: not present — omit.\n\n**date_issued**: The signature date 08.10.2014 → \"2014-10-08\".\n\n**oiml_issuer_id**: not present — omit. (\"R3-0033\" is a form number, skip.)\n\n**revision_history**: none — omit.\n\n**test_reports**: No. PTB-1.5-4071210, includes 2164 pages, role \"test_report\" (cert says \"the associated Test Reports No. PTB-1.5-4071210 that includes 2164 pages\"). role: \"test_report\" — schema options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. It's the Test Report for R49-3. Use \"test_report\". pages: 2164. date: not given.\n\n**footnotes**: none.\n\n**components**: The water meter is a complete instrument \"with mechanical indicating device\" — not decomposed into named components. Omit components.\n\n**model_family**: family_name: null (no explicit family name). models: 24.\n\nWait — should models be at type-designation level instead? The certified types are ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4. One could argue models[] should be the 4 types and everything else config_level. Hmm. The tables group ET1,ET3,ET4 together and ET2 separately — meaning within a table, the characteristics apply to multiple type designations. So \"model\" in my structure = (type-set × DN × Q3 × T × orientation). That's a valid notion of variant. The model_family.models entries then reference multiple type designations. I think that's the most information-preserving mapping.\n\nAlternatively, expand to individual types: ET1_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H, ET3_..., ET4_... tripling some entries — total 6×8+... hmm: tables with 3 types → each type gets a model. Count: tables per type-set: {E1,E3,E4}: T1,T2,T5,T6,T13,T14,T17,T18 = 8 tables ×3 = 24; {E2}: T3,T4,T7,T8,T15,T16,T19,T20 = 8; {E1-4}: T9,T10,T11,T12,T21,T22,T23,T24 = 8 ×4 = 32. Total 84 model entries. Too many. Keep 24 with applicable_types attribute.\n\nNow let me draft the JSON.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"B\",\n    \"page_total\": 11,\n    \"member_state\": \"Germany\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2014-10-08\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Dr. G. Wendt\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head of Department\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Diehl Metering GmbH\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Industriestraße 13\", \"91522 Ansbach\", \"GERMANY\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Diehl Metering GmbH\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Industriestraße 13\", \"91522 Ansbach\", \"GERMANY\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"],\n    \"description\": \"Water meter with mechanical indicating device\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [...24...]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"q2_over_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"minimum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 0.3, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      \"minimum_straight_length_inlet\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"},\n      \"minimum_straight_length_outlet\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": []\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...25...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2006,\n    \"scheme\": \"B\"\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\"id\": \"PTB-1.5-4071210\", \"pages\": 2164, \"role\": \"test_report\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFor minimum_admissible_pressure: \"0,3 bar (0,03MPa)\" — value 0.3 bar. Should I add unit_id \"u:bar\"? Leave null per rule 3 caution. Actually bar is common; unitsdb has bar presumably. Leave null to be safe? The rule says leave null unless certain. I'll leave null everywhere.\n\nNow build the 24 models. Let me create a consistent id format:\n\n- Types joined by \"+\"? e.g. \"ET1+ET3+ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H\". Hmm, \"+\" in ids is fine. Or use \"/\" — \"ET1/ET3/ET4\". I'll use \"-\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3_2.5_T30_H\". Underscores separating fields, hyphens within types. Q3 with decimal: \"Q3=2.5\". Let me use format: \"ET1-ET3-ET4__DN15__Q3=2.5m³/h__T30__H\"? Slightly ugly. Simpler: \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_T30_H\". I'll go with \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3-2.5_T30_H\".\n\nHmm, but hyphen-minus in \"Q3-2.5\" reads as dash not equals. Acceptable. Actually use \"Q3=2.5\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H\". Fine.\n\nModels list:\n\nM01: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H — label \"ET1, ET3, ET4 — DN15, Q3 2,5 m³/h, T30 (0,1–30 °C), horizontal\"\nM02: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_V — \"…vertical (rising/falling pipe, V 90° and V 45°)\"\nM03: ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H\nM04: ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_V\nM05: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6_T30_H\nM06: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6_T30_V\nM07: ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6_T30_H\nM08: ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6_T30_V\nM09: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5_T30_H\nM10: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5_T30_V\nM11: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0_T30_H\nM12: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0_T30_V\nM13: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T90_H\nM14: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T90_V\nM15: ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5_T90_H\nM16: ET2_DN15_Q3=2.5_T90_V\nM17: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6_T90_H\nM18: ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=1.6_T90_V\nM19: ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6_T90_H\nM20: ET2_DN15_Q3=1.6_T90_V\nM21: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5_T90_H\nM22: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=2.5_T90_V\nM23: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0_T90_H\nM24: ET1-ET2-ET3-ET4_DN20_Q3=4.0_T90_V\n\nWait — is T30/T90 fabrication? The cert gives temperature ranges 0.1–30°C and 30–90°C. R49-1 defines T30 (0.1–30°C) and T90 (30–90°C) temperature classes. Using them as labels is an interpretation but a standard one. To be safe, label as \"T30 (0.1–30°C)\" in labels and use temperature_class attribute with value \"T30\"/\"T90\"? Hmm, risk of fabricating. Alternatively temperature_class attribute value could be the range itself. I'll include both: \"temperature_class\" with \"T30\"/\"T90\" (standard R49 designation — justifiable) and \"water_temperature_range\" with the explicit ranges from the cert. Actually, to minimize fabrication risk, I could skip \"temperature_class\" and keep model_id containing T30/T90 as shorthand explained in label. I'll keep temperature_class since T30/T90 is the R49 designation for exactly these ranges; it aids consumers. Hmm... The label can make it transparent: label includes the explicit range. OK.\n\nOrientation labels: \"H\" = horizontal; \"V\" — cert text: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° und V 45°\". So V variants allow vertical mounting with rising or falling flow at 90° and 45°. I'll set orientation attribute value: \"H\" or \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\".\n\nmodel_level entries (attribute, unit, values for all 24 models):\n\n1. nominal_diameter_dn (mm): 15 for M01–M08, M13–M20; 20 for M09–M12, M21–M24.\n2. q3 (m³/h): 2.5 (M01–M04, M09, M10, M13–M16, M21, M22); 1.6 (M05–M08, M17–M20); 4.0 (M11, M12, M23, M24).\n3. q4 (m³/h): 3.13 for Q3=2.5 (DN15 & DN20), 2.0 for Q3=1.6 DN15, 5.0 for Q3=4.0 DN20.\n4. applicable_types (string): \"ET1, ET3, ET4\" / \"ET2\" / \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4\".\n5. temperature_class: T30/T90.\n6. water_temperature_range (°C): {min:0.1,max:30} T30; {min:30,max:90} T90.\n7. maximum_admissible_error_upper_zone (%): 2 for T30; 3 for T90.\n8. maximum_admissible_error_lower_zone (%): 5 all.\n9. orientation: \"H\"/\"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\".\n10. pressure_loss_class: per dn/q3: DN15 1.6→25; DN15 2.5→63; DN20 2.5→40; DN20 4.0→63.\n\nEach model_level value object: {\"model\": id, \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., } — wait, schema shape: model_level[]: {attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}}. In the R137 example, they used \"unit_symbol\" in model_level entries (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). The schema yaml says \"unit\" but example says \"unit_symbol\". I'll use \"unit_symbol\" per the examples. Values: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...}. For range values include unit_symbol? In example, unit at attribute level. I'll put unit_symbol at attribute level, values just {model, value}. For string values (orientation, applicable_types, temperature_class) unit_symbol null.\n\nNow matrix_tables. 25 total (24 flow + 1 pressure). Each flow table:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Flow range — <model_id>\",\n  \"columns\": [\"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [ {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 25, \"q1\": 0.1, \"q2\": 0.16, \"q3\": 2.5, \"q4\": 3.13, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6}, ... ],\n  \"description\": \"Model ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3=2.5_T30_H (ET1, ET3, ET4; DN15; Q3 2.5 m³/h; 0.1–30 °C; accuracy ±2 % (Q2≤Q≤Q4), ±5 % (Q1≤Q<Q2); orientation H).\"\n}\n\nPressure table:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Pressure range and pressure loss\",\n  \"columns\": [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3\", \"pmin\", \"pmax\", \"pressure_loss_class\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3\": 1.6, \"pmin\": \"0.3 bar (0.03 MPa)\", \"pmax\": \"10 bar (1.0 MPa)\", \"pressure_loss_class\": 25},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm — pmin/pmax as strings with units embedded? Better: separate: rows keyed by dn/q3 with pressure_loss_class; pmin/pmax already in type_level. Let me structure: columns [\"dn\", \"q3\", \"pressure_loss_class\"], column_units [\"mm\", \"m³/h\", null], rows: [{dn:15, q3:1.6, pressure_loss_class: 25}, {dn:15, q3:2.5, pressure_loss_class: 63}, {dn:20, q3:2.5, pressure_loss_class: 40}, {dn:20, q3:4.0, pressure_loss_class: 63}], description mentions pmin/pmax apply to all (0.3 bar / 10 bar).\n\nWait, careful with \"ΔP 25\" — is it the class or max pressure loss in mbar? Δp class 25 ↔ 0.25 bar? Hmm: For water meters (ISO 4064), pressure loss classes: Δp 25, Δp 40, Δp 63, corresponding to 0.25 bar, 0.40 bar, 0.63 bar at Q3. Yes (standard classes 25/40/63). The cert just writes \"ΔP 25\". I'll record value 25, attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" (also add Δp symbol maybe \"delta_p_class\"). Use attribute name \"pressure_loss_class_delta_p\". I'll name it \"pressure_loss_class\" and note symbol ΔP in description.\n\nNow type_level: also maybe \"q3_over_q1\"? varies. No.\n\nAlso \"minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\", \"minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\". Names: \"minimum_straight_length_inlet\", \"minimum_straight_length_outlet\".\n\nNow let me also double check page 2 signature table: Issuing Authority Dr. G. Wendt Head of Department 08.10.2014; CIML Member Dr. R. Schwartz Vice-President 08.10.2014. Date issued = 2014-10-08. Good.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\".\n\nShould scheme be \"B\"? The OIML-CS came later (2017); this 2014 cert is under the \"OIML Certificate System for Measuring Instruments\"... Actually before OIML CS, there was the \"OIML Certificate System\" which issued OIML certificates of conformity — this maps to Scheme B in OIML-CS (certificates of conformity issued by issuing authorities based on test reports). I'll use \"B\".\n\nNow, let me write all rows carefully.\n\nTable M01 rows (T30, H, DN15, Q3 2.5):\n- ratio 25: q1 0.100, q2 0.160\n- ratio 31.5: q1 0.079, q2 0.127\n- ratio 40: q1 0.063, q2 0.100\n- ratio 50: q1 0.050, q2 0.080\n- ratio 63: q1 0.040, q2 0.063\n- ratio 80: q1 0.031, q2 0.050\n- ratio 100: q1 0.025, q2 0.040\n- ratio 125: q1 0.020, q2 0.032\nq3 2.5, q4 3.13 all.\n\nTable M02 (T30, V): ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50 with q1 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050; q2 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080.\n\nTable M03 (ET2 T30 H): ratios 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100; q1 0.100, 0.079, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040, 0.031, 0.025; q2 0.160, 0.127, 0.100, 0.080, 0.063, 0.050, 0.040.\n\nTable M04 (ET2 T30 V): ratios 25, 31.5; q1 0.100, 0.079